## WHAT WORKED, worth keeping

### Lagrange generalizes to ANY subgroup by a model — `inSubgroup` is a model source

Worth stating because the opposite is the natural reading. `std/group/coset.b4m` and
`std/group/lagrange.b4m` are written over ONE predicate `inSubgroup` with the three
criteria as axioms, which looks like "this chapter is about one fixed subgroup".

It is not. The predicate is a MODEL SOURCE: map `coset.inSubgroup` onto any predicate
whose three criteria you can prove, and the coset algebra AND the counting theorem
transfer. Verified in `tests/cases/model_lagrange_at_a_subgroup.b4m` on the CYCLIC
subgroup — `inCyclic(x) iff inGenerated(gen, x)`, criteria from
`std/group/generated.b4m`, then `model CyclicSubgroup` and `lagrange.lagrange`
transfers in one line.

Same lesson as the A/N section above, twice in one chapter: **a fixed predicate (or
constant) plus axioms is a model source, and without a fixture demonstrating the
model it is indistinguishable from a restriction.** Both now have one.

GOTCHA when writing the transferred statement: it must be in the MAPPED vocabulary.
Citing `coset.inCoset(g, g)` fails — the `define` expands with the UNMAPPED
`inSubgroup`, so the transfer derives the `inCyclic` form and the step must claim
that. A `define` is not model-mapped by the mapping of the predicate inside it.

### Top-down with `hole` paid for itself on the block decomposition

Writing the `lagrange` ENDPOINT first, against four `hole` stubs, settled every
intermediate signature before any of them was proved — and the holes it did NOT cite
told me which lemmas fed the induction rather than the theorem. The one interface I
got wrong (four lemmas taking `listsCosetReps(r, succ(c))` when the induction only
ever holds it at a fixed `k`) surfaced as a shape mismatch at first assembly, not
after writing proofs against it.

Confirms the rule in `agents/2b4m-cheatsheet.md`: bottom-up when the statement is
GIVEN (Judson's lemmas), top-down when the statement is YOURS to invent (the plumbing
between them). A hole still needs its FULL statement written, which is most of the
design work — that is the point, not a cost.

### A `hole` cannot be stubbed mid-proof, and that forced a better factoring

Wanting to stub the middle of `blocksExtendByOne` (I cited a `blocksExtendByOneCore`
that would have needed the whole local context threaded into it), I had to instead
split the three `listsTheBlocksBelow` clauses into three top-level lemmas, each
taking the concatenation's two entrywise agreements plus only the clauses it uses.
That is the better factoring — each clause argues on a different index split and they
share nothing else — and the restriction is what produced it.

